      "A `const` object can still have mutable properties because the binding is stable while the referenced value remains mutable. Freeze or copy data only when the domain requires it; neither `const` nor shallow freezing recursively protects nested objects.",
      "The temporal dead zone protects code from reading a lexical binding before initialization. Treat the resulting error as a design signal about ordering or circular dependency, not as a reason to replace every declaration with `var`.",
      "When shared mutation is intentional, put it behind a function that enforces invariants and names the change. Returning internal arrays or objects lets callers bypass those rules, so copy or expose read-only views at ownership boundaries.",
      "Defaults in destructuring apply only to `undefined`, not to null or other false-like values. Rename ambiguous fields while binding and validate the whole external object first so concise syntax does not hide a weak input contract."
